\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Onym data and metadata flow. Each member holds a BLS12-381 secret key and produces a 192-byte Groth16 proof $\pi $ locally; one member submits the update tuple via a public relayer that pays the transaction fee. The Soroban contract verifies the proof using three BLS12-381 pairings and writes the new 32-byte commitment $C_{\mathsf  {new}}$ and incremented epoch to storage. Italicized callouts mark the metadata each non-member party in the path observes.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:arch}{{1}{4}{Onym data and metadata flow. Each member holds a BLS12-381 secret key and produces a 192-byte Groth16 proof $\pi $ locally; one member submits the update tuple via a public relayer that pays the transaction fee. The Soroban contract verifies the proof using three BLS12-381 pairings and writes the new 32-byte commitment $\Cnew $ and incremented epoch to storage. Italicized callouts mark the metadata each non-member party in the path observes}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces P1: the bug and its fix. The original relation $R_{\mathsf  {Mem}}$ bound only the current state $(C, \epsilon )$; the contract wrote whatever \texttt  {new\_commitment} appeared in the call, leaving it controllable by anyone in the network path. The fix promotes $C_{\mathsf  {new}}$ to a public input of a new relation $R_{\mathsf  {Upd}}$, and the public-input-hashing modification of~\cite  {baghery2021} adds simulation-extractability so the proof cannot be re-randomized into a different accepting proof.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:p1}{{2}{4}{P1: the bug and its fix. The original relation $\Rmem $ bound only the current state $(C, \epsilon )$; the contract wrote whatever \texttt {new\_commitment} appeared in the call, leaving it controllable by anyone in the network path. The fix promotes $\Cnew $ to a public input of a new relation $\Rupd $, and the public-input-hashing modification of~\cite {baghery2021} adds simulation-extractability so the proof cannot be re-randomized into a different accepting proof}{figure.2}{}}
